@node Overview
@chapter Overview

@samp{agent.el} collects the Emacs integration layer for AI coding command-line tools. It provides a shared session model, a unified transient menu, notification handling, terminal helpers, backend modules for Claude Code and Codex, and an optional chief-of-staff loop.

The package is split into a small backend-agnostic core and focused modules:

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Module
@tab Role
@item @samp{agent.el}
@tab Core: backend registry, session identity and state machine, session keys and teardown, unified orchestration commands (handoff, restart, exit, resume, branch navigation, skills, audits, backtrace debugging), transient menu, alerts, theme sync
@item @samp{agent-account.el}
@tab Multi-account support shared by all backends: selection, persistence, per-account config homes, symlinked shared state
@item @samp{agent-usage.el}
@tab Per-account usage store and poller shared by all backends
@item @samp{agent-capture.el}
@tab Persisted Org prompt capture, keyed to the session it targets
@item @samp{agent-project.el}
@tab Account-scoped project sources: registry and path-pattern candidates, model ranking, and the project prompt for things that carry no directory of their own
@item @samp{agent-slack.el}
@tab Slack-message-to-project routing: reads the message at point as text with no directory of its own, leaving the project to the shared project reader
@item @samp{agent-forge.el}
@tab Forge-notification-to-project routing: starts a session in the working tree of the repository the notification belongs to
@item @samp{agent-mu4e.el}
@tab Email-to-project routing: reads the mu4e message at point as a maildir path, leaving the project to the shared project reader
@item @samp{agent-snippet.el}
@tab Yasnippet TAB expansion and snippet insertion in eat-based session buffers
@item @samp{agent-todo.el}
@tab Org TODO workflows that work with any backend: batch processing of a list of entries and routing the entry at point to a session
@item @samp{agent-claude.el}
@tab Claude Code backend: integrates @samp{claude-code.el} with status and usage polling, hook setup, and @code{agent-claude-mode}
@item @samp{agent-claude-cli.el}
@tab Quarantined undocumented Claude CLI conventions: Keychain credential naming, the usage API endpoint, the @samp{~/.claude/projects/} directory encoding, transcript JSONL parsing, and the @samp{.claude.json} merge engine
@item @samp{agent-codex.el}
@tab Codex backend: integrates @samp{codex.el} with theme sync, @samp{codex exec} execution, @samp{config.toml} helpers, modeline helpers, and @code{agent-codex-mode}
@item @samp{agent-chief.el}
@tab Inspectable chief-of-staff loop: Emacs owns timers, state, context collection, and notifications, while Claude Code or Codex provides one-shot judgment on each tick
@end multitable

The main workflow is to load @samp{agent.el}, require the backend modules you use, and enable their minor modes (see @ref{Backend minor modes}); loading a backend module installs nothing by itself. Once the Claude and Codex modules are active, @samp{M-x agent-menu} and @samp{M-x agent-select-session} operate across both tools. Sessions are listed with their account in a column of its own, highlighted when waiting for input, and disambiguated by project and instance name when several sessions share a directory.

The package depends on @samp{transient} for menus, @samp{consult} for completion, @samp{claude-code.el} for the Claude backend, and @samp{codex.el} for the Codex backend. All four are declared in the package metadata so that the package manager builds @samp{agent} with them available; the backend modules hard-require their respective backend at load time. Optional integrations use packages such as @samp{yasnippet}, @samp{doom-modeline}, @samp{gptel}, @samp{agent-log}, and @samp{monet} when those are available.

