@node Session identity
@section Session identity

@findex agent-session
@findex agent-session-buffer-name
Every live session buffer carries a buffer-local @code{agent-session} struct, captured once when the session starts. Its slots are @code{backend} (the backend symbol), @code{account} (the account name, or nil for the default), @code{directory} (the abbreviated project directory), @code{instance} (the instance name, or nil), and @code{id} (the CLI session id, or nil until known). The function @code{agent-session} returns the struct for a buffer, and @code{agent-session-buffer-name} derives the canonical buffer name from the identity. All orchestration code reads identity from this struct; nothing parses buffer names.

@findex agent-session-buffers
@vindex agent-session-id-functions
The @code{id} slot holds the backend's native session id and is maintained for the whole session lifetime: a non-fork resume seeds it in @code{agent-start-session}, the Claude backend records the @code{session_id} reported by every status poll (so a branch switch, which changes the id, updates it), and the Codex backend records the id @code{codex-session-identity} reports on every CLI hook event. A fresh session therefore acquires its id within one status poll interval (Claude) or at its first hook event or prompt submission, whichever comes first (Codex); a fresh app-server Codex session knows its thread id before any CLI hook event fires, so the submission hook is the earliest per-buffer signal. Whenever the recorded id changes, the abnormal hook @code{agent-session-id-functions} runs with the session buffer, so optional integrations — such as the Agent Log bridge — can observe identity without reading private variables. The public surface for such integrations is @code{agent-session}, the @code{agent-session-id} accessor, @code{agent-session-buffers} (all live session buffers across backends), and @code{agent-session-display-state}.

@node Session state machine
@section Session state machine

@findex agent-session-event
Each session buffer tracks its readiness in the buffer-local variable @code{agent--session-state}, which is one of @code{unknown}, @code{busy}, @code{awaiting-input}, or @code{closing}. Buffers start as @code{unknown} and stay there until a session event arrives, so a state that was never observed is never presented as one that was; the session switcher renders such sessions with @code{agent-unknown} and @code{agent-jump-to-waiting} skips them. All transitions go through @code{agent-session-event}, which accepts the events @code{stop}, @code{idle-prompt}, @code{blocked}, @code{submit}, @code{activity}, and @code{exit-request}. An @code{activity} event marks the session busy on evidence that it is working. It exists because turns the user did not type in Emacs — one driven by remote control, a scheduled task, or a resumed background job — would otherwise be unobservable, leaving the session displayed as waiting while it works. Claude Code has no busy/idle state of its own, so the backend assembles one from its hooks. The bundled @samp{notify-emacs-state.sh} wrapper forwards the @samp{UserPromptSubmit}, @samp{PreToolUse}, @samp{PostToolUse}, @samp{PostToolUseFailure}, and @samp{SubagentStart} hooks as @code{activity}, whoever started the turn, and @samp{StopFailure} — a turn ended by an API error, which fires no @samp{Stop} hook — as @code{stop}. The tool hooks also return a session to busy once the user answers a permission dialog. Each forwarded event carries its send time, and @code{agent-claude--stale-hook-event-p} discards one sent before the session last began waiting, because background delivery can land a turn's final tool event after its stop event. A turn Claude starts without a prompt and runs without tools fires none of these hooks, so the status-line poll remains as a fallback: Claude's status line reports a fresh @code{prompt_id} for every turn whatever its origin, and @code{agent-claude--detect-turn-start} emits @code{activity} when the identifier changes, within one poll interval, @code{agent-claude-status-interval}. A turn shorter than that interval both begins and ends between polls, and its @code{stop} event has already landed by the time the change is seen, so that case is detected and skipped rather than stranding the session as busy. Interrupting a turn fires no hook at all, so an escape sent with @code{claude-code-send-escape} to a busy session schedules @code{agent-claude--check-interrupt}: after @code{agent-claude-interrupt-check-delay} seconds, if the session is still busy with no state change since the escape and its terminal shows the idle input prompt, the session is marked @code{blocked}. An interrupt from outside Emacs, such as through remote control, is not detected until the next turn ends. A @code{blocked} event marks the session as waiting on the user without firing a ready alert, and is used for permission and elicitation dialogs: Claude reaches those from inside a turn with a tool call still in flight, so nothing else reports them as waiting even though they proceed only once the user answers. Backends translate raw CLI notifications into these events and never set session state directly. A @code{stop} or @code{idle-prompt} event first offers the transition to the before-exit chain; when the chain consumes it, ready alerts and display refreshes are suppressed. Ready alerts fire only for @code{idle-prompt} events, and a @code{submit} event delivered while the session is already busy is ignored, because backend submission hooks can fire more than once per submission.

@node Session teardown and leak policy
@section Session teardown and leak policy

Session resource cleanup has a single owner: @code{agent--session-teardown} runs a session buffer's @code{agent--teardown-functions} exactly once, whether the buffer is killed first or the CLI process exits first. Backends and features register their per-session resources (status poll timers, monet servers, before-exit chains) as teardown functions instead of installing their own kill-buffer hooks. A session key has buffer lifetime instead: assigning one atomically installs its buffer-kill cleanup, while session teardown can release it early after process exit. Claude buffers also adopt any already-running Monet server when @code{agent-claude-mode} is enabled or reloaded. The monet websocket server needs one extra guard: when the Claude client disconnects, @code{monet--on-close-server} drops the session from monet's own table before teardown can look it up, so an @samp{:after} advice closes the listening server at that moment rather than letting it escape to the GC safety net. Resources that escape teardown are not silently reaped: safety nets report each one through a @samp{Warnings} buffer entry tagged @samp{agent}, naming the leaked resource. A leak warning therefore indicates a lifecycle bug worth reporting, not routine noise. The Monet GC sweep identifies servers by ownership, not by process name: Agent registers every server process it sees monet create or adopt, and the sweep only ever reaps registered servers that have lost their monet session. Websocket servers belonging to other packages (atomic-chrome, org-roam-ui) share the generic @samp{websocket server on port N} process name but are invisible to the sweep and can never be reported or killed as monet leaks.

@node Account model
@section Account model

@findex agent-account-select
@findex agent-account-init
Multi-account state lives in @samp{agent-account.el} and is keyed by backend. Each backend declares an accounts alist mapping account names to per-account configuration homes (@code{agent-claude-accounts}, @code{agent-codex-accounts}), a persisted selection file that survives Emacs restarts (@code{agent-claude-account-file}, @code{agent-codex-account-file}), and the environment variable that points the CLI at the selected home: @samp{CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR} for Claude Code and @samp{CODEX_HOME} for Codex. Account homes keep credentials separate, while shared state (settings, hooks, skills, sessions, history) is symlinked from the backend's canonical home so it remains available after switching accounts; conflicting account-local files are backed up to timestamped @samp{*.agent-backup-*} siblings first. Codex hook trust is keyed by the lexical @samp{hooks.json} path rather than the symlink target, so account sync copies the canonical path's persisted trust state to the account path. It copies existing hashes rather than computing new ones; a changed canonical hook therefore remains untrusted. Any sync failure aborts session startup instead of letting a session run with missing or stale policy. @code{agent-account-select} persists a choice and syncs the account home, and @code{agent-account-init} prepares or repairs a home. The account infix in @code{agent-menu} always asks which backend to change before it asks for an account; it never silently infers the backend from the buffer where the menu happened to be opened. When the merge engine reads a home's @samp{.claude.json}, a parse failure is retried over increasing delays before warning, since a running CLI rewrites the file frequently and a read can land mid-write; a failure that persists is warned about once, with the invalid bytes preserved in a snapshot file so a torn read can be told apart from real corruption. Writes go to a temporary file that is renamed into place, so concurrent readers never observe a truncated @samp{.claude.json}. The account a session was started under is recorded in its @code{agent-session} struct, so restart and handoff preserve it even after the global selection changes.

@findex agent-account-property
@findex agent-account-route
An accounts entry is either @samp{(NAME . HOME)} or @samp{(NAME :home HOME :pool POOL ...)}. The plist form carries arbitrary further properties, read with @code{agent-account-property}, for integrations that need per-account settings beyond the home. Accounts sharing a @code{:pool} are interchangeable: they hold the same configuration and differ only in whose quota they spend. The selection prompt lists pools ahead of accounts, and selecting a pool persists the pool name, so @code{agent-account-current} may answer with a pool; @code{agent-account-resolve} always answers with a concrete account, routing a pool selection through @code{agent-account-route}. Selecting a pool syncs every member's home, so routing never has to touch the filesystem. A member chosen by name pins that account as before. A pool may carry the same name as one of its members — convenient when an existing selection file names the first of what became several equivalent accounts — in which case the name is listed once and selects the pool, so that member can no longer be pinned by name.

@vindex agent-account-route-hysteresis
@findex agent-account-usage-score
Routing skips members whose credentials are missing, then sets aside members whose latest fresh usage reading (@ref{Account usage}) flags them as limited or shows either window at 100%. The rest are ranked by @code{agent-account-usage-score}: the larger of the weekly and session percentages, with the weekly share breaking ties, so lower is better; a member with no fresh reading ranks after every member with one. The member the pool was last routed to is kept unless a sibling beats it by @code{agent-account-route-hysteresis} points (default @samp{10}), so near-equal members do not alternate between consecutive sessions — except that a current member with unknown usage never holds off a sibling with a known score. When every member is limited, the one whose window resets soonest is returned, and the echo area says so, so the session start can still report the limit. A route that changes hands is announced with the chosen member's usage; a resolution that keeps the previous member stays silent, since routing also runs for background processes and would otherwise flood the echo area. Every routing decision is followed by a refresh of the pool's readings. A restart of a session whose account belongs to the selected pool reruns routing without prompting, so an exhausted member can be replaced by one with capacity. Both backends share session history between account homes, so the conversation is preserved.

@findex agent-account-login
Credentials are part of the account model too. A backend may declare the account-local credentials file inside each home (@samp{auth.json} for Codex) and the CLI arguments of its login flow. @code{agent-account-login} — bound to @samp{a l} in @code{agent-menu} — runs that login flow with the selected account's environment, so the credentials always land in the right account home; running the bare CLI login in a terminal would write to the backend's default home instead. The command shows the login output, including the authentication URL, in a dedicated buffer and reports success or failure when the flow finishes. Session starts guard on the same declaration: when an account's credentials file is missing — after the in-session logout command, or when tokens were removed out of band — @code{agent-start-session} refuses to spawn a session that could only fail to authenticate and points at @code{agent-account-login} instead. Backends that do not declare a credentials file (Claude Code stores credentials in the macOS Keychain) are assumed logged in and never blocked.

@node Account usage
@section Account usage

@vindex agent-usage-interval
@vindex agent-usage-max-interval
@vindex agent-usage-cache-file
@vindex agent-usage-stale-after
@findex agent-usage-get
@findex agent-usage-fetch
Per-account usage lives in @samp{agent-usage.el} and is keyed by backend and account. A backend that can report usage declares a @code{:usage-fetch} slot: a function called with an account name and a callback, which it calls with a normalized plist or nil. The plist carries @code{:session-pct} and @code{:weekly-pct} (percent of the short and long windows used), @code{:session-reset} and @code{:weekly-reset} (reset times as float seconds), @code{:limited} (non-nil when the backend refuses further requests), and @code{:fetched-at}. Claude reads the undocumented @samp{api/oauth/usage} endpoint with the Keychain OAuth token; Codex reads the ChatGPT @samp{wham/usage} endpoint with the access token and account id stored in the account home's @samp{auth.json}, classifying a window of at least a day as weekly and a shorter one as the session window, so a plan with a single weekly window reports only that. @code{agent-usage-get} answers the latest reading and @code{agent-usage-fetch} refreshes one account.

One poller serves every backend. It starts with the first session of any backend and stops when the last one is torn down, polling every account that has a live session plus every member of every pool, so routing has data for idle members too. @code{agent-usage-interval} defaults to @samp{300} seconds and doubles after a failed fetch up to @code{agent-usage-max-interval}, which defaults to @samp{900}; a success resets it. Readings are written to @code{agent-usage-cache-file} after every fetch and read back on first use, so a fresh Emacs can route before its first poll completes; a reading older than @code{agent-usage-stale-after} seconds no longer counts for routing. The Claude status helpers @code{agent-claude-status-session-usage} and friends read this store and keep answering ISO timestamps for the reset times. Claude usage requests report synchronous network failures, including DNS lookup errors, as failed fetches without entering the debugger. Only stale URL process write failures are retried immediately, once.

@findex agent-usage-show
@code{agent-usage-show} — bound to @samp{a u} in @code{agent-menu} — lists every configured account, live-session account, and pool member in a @samp{*agent-usage*} buffer with its pool, session and weekly percentages, weekly reset time, limit flag, and the age of the reading, then fetches fresh readings and re-renders as each arrives; @samp{g} refreshes again. Configured accounts appear even without a live session; backends without configured or active accounts show their default account. Background polling remains limited to live-session accounts and pool members. The limit flag and the percentages are what routing acts on, so the buffer shows at a glance why a pool went to the member it did.

@node Backend minor modes
@section Backend minor modes

@findex agent-claude-mode
@findex agent-codex-mode
The global minor modes @code{agent-claude-mode} and @code{agent-codex-mode} own every hook, advice, timer, and notification handler their backend installs. Loading @samp{agent-claude.el} or @samp{agent-codex.el} installs nothing; the modes must be enabled explicitly. Disabling a mode removes its hooks and advice symmetrically and restores the backend's previous notification function, leaving running sessions untouched. A typical setup:

@lisp
(use-package agent
  :config
  (require 'agent-claude)
  (require 'agent-codex)
  (require 'agent-chief)
  (agent-claude-mode 1)
  (agent-codex-mode 1))
@end lisp

