@node Shared session behavior
@section Shared session behavior

@vindex agent-protect-buffers
@vindex agent-warn-kill-with-branches
@vindex agent-branch-worktree-directory
@vindex agent-alert-on-ready
@vindex agent-alert-style
@vindex agent-alert-sound
@vindex agent-alert-sound-player
@vindex agent-backtrace-file
@vindex agent-handoff-files
@vindex agent-audit-skills
@vindex agent-audit-project-directories
@vindex agent-debug-backtrace-model
@vindex agent-debug-backtrace-backend
@vindex agent-prompt-capture-directory
@vindex agent-prompt-capture-auto-save-delay
@vindex agent-sync-theme
@vindex agent-sigwinch-delay
@vindex agent-session-annotation-max-width
The user option @code{agent-protect-buffers} is a boolean that defaults to @code{t}. When enabled, killing a live AI session buffer prompts for confirmation through @code{agent-protect-buffer}. Buffers whose processes have already exited are allowed to die without the prompt.

The user option @code{agent-warn-kill-with-branches} is a boolean that defaults to @code{t}. When enabled, killing a session that other sessions were branched from asks a second confirmation naming how many branches it has, after the ordinary kill-protection prompt. The check scans only the sessions that could descend from the one being killed, and any failure to scan allows the kill, so a session never becomes unkillable because its transcripts moved. It replaces @code{agent-claude-warn-kill-with-branches}, which is kept as an obsolete alias; that option was declared but never read by any code, so setting it had no effect until the check described here existed.

The user option @code{agent-branch-worktree-directory} names the base directory for the git worktrees @code{agent-create-branch} creates with a prefix argument (@ref{Branch navigation}). It defaults to a location under the cache directory, away from cloud sync, and replaces @code{agent-claude-fork-worktree-directory}, which is kept as an obsolete alias. The default path still ends in the literal segment @samp{claude-worktrees}, unchanged from the Claude-only original so that worktrees created before the rename are still found where they were left.

The user option @code{agent-alert-on-ready} is a boolean that defaults to @code{nil}. When enabled, backend notification handlers can call @code{agent-notify} to emit visual and/or audible alerts when a session is waiting for input. Codex's own terminal-bell notification path is routed through the same alert setting. Visual alerts preserve the current echo-area message even when the selected @samp{alert.el} style runs a synchronous notifier process. @code{agent-alert-style} selects @code{visual}, @code{sound}, or @code{both}; it defaults to @code{both}. @code{agent-alert-sound} defaults to @code{nil}; set it to a sound file to enable sound alerts. @code{agent-alert-sound-player} optionally names an external sound player when Emacs sound support is unavailable.

The user option @code{agent-backtrace-file} controls where @code{agent-save-backtrace} and @code{agent-debug-backtrace} write Emacs backtraces. It defaults to a file in @code{temporary-file-directory}.

The unified orchestration commands share one set of options across backends. @code{agent-handoff-files} maps backend symbols to the handoff files read by @code{agent-handoff}. @code{agent-audit-skills} lists the audit skills run by @code{agent-audit-project}, and @code{agent-audit-project-directories} stores completion candidates for the audit directory prompt. @code{agent-debug-backtrace-model} and @code{agent-debug-backtrace-backend} configure the @samp{gptel} request used by @code{agent-debug-backtrace} to identify likely culprit packages from an Emacs backtrace.

Slack-message routing has no options of its own any more. The model and @samp{gptel} backend that pick a project from the message are the shared @code{agent-project-ranking-model} and @code{agent-project-ranking-backend} (@ref{Project sources}), and @code{agent-act-on-slack-message-model} and @code{agent-act-on-slack-message-backend} are obsolete aliases of them. The project candidates come from @code{agent-project-sources}, so a registry that used to be named by @code{agent-epoch-project-registry-file} now belongs in that alist: that option is obsolete and nothing reads it, which means a configuration setting it alone routes against the default source list instead of the registry. Loading @samp{agent-project} warns when that option still holds a value, because marking a variable obsolete warns only code that mentions it and says nothing to a configuration that merely sets it, which is the whole of what such a configuration does. @code{agent-epoch-projects-root} is an obsolete alias of @code{agent-project-registry-root} and still resolves the registry's relative paths. The function @code{agent-epoch-project-candidates}, which read that registry into project plists, is replaced by @code{agent-project-candidates}, which reads every source an account has and answers in a different shape. The old name is marked obsolete but no longer defined, so a configuration still calling it is warned at byte-compile time which name replaced it, and fails if it runs. All three renamings are declared in @samp{agent-project.el} ahead of the options they rename, because a setting made before the package loads — which is what @code{use-package}'s @code{:custom} does — reaches the new name only while that name is still unbound.

The user option @code{agent-prompt-capture-directory} controls where @code{agent-capture-prompt} stores per-session Org files. Each file is keyed by backend, account, project directory, and session instance, so prompts are linked to the session they target and remain available after Emacs restarts. @code{agent-prompt-capture-auto-save-delay} controls the idle delay before capture buffers are saved automatically.

The user option @code{agent-sync-theme} is a boolean that defaults to @code{nil}. When enabled, theme changes in Emacs are persisted through each registered backend's theme-sync handler. This updates configuration files for new or restarted CLI sessions without sending theme commands into active terminal conversations.

The user option @code{agent-sigwinch-delay} is a number of seconds and defaults to @samp{0.5}. It controls how long @code{agent-fix-rendering} waits before sending @samp{SIGWINCH} to a terminal process. Increase it if an AI CLI reads the terminal size before the @samp{eat} window has settled.

The user option @code{agent-session-annotation-max-width} caps the width of session annotations in the session switcher (@ref{Shared commands}). It defaults to @code{nil}, which applies no cap beyond the fit to the frame. An integer narrows annotations to at most that many columns; it never widens them past what the frame holds, since the frame fit applies either way. Text longer than the resulting width is truncated with an ellipsis. A cap of @samp{0} leaves no room at all, and every session then renders as the bare label it would have without an annotation, rather than a padded label followed by nothing.

Snippet helpers use @samp{yasnippet} and @samp{consult-yasnippet} only when those optional packages are loaded.

@node Project sources
@section Project sources

@vindex agent-project-sources
@vindex agent-project-registry-root
@vindex agent-project-ranking-model
@vindex agent-project-ranking-backend
@findex agent-project-candidates
@findex agent-project-read
The @samp{agent-project} module enumerates the projects a session can be started in when the thing the session acts on carries no directory of its own. The user option @code{agent-project-sources} is an alist of (REGEXP . SOURCES). The regexp is searched for anywhere in the account name of the backend that will run the session — anchor it with @samp{\`} and @samp{\'} to match a whole name, since a bare @samp{"epoch"} also matches an account called @samp{epoch-personal} — and the first matching entry wins, so an empty regexp is a catch-all that also covers a backend with no account selected. It defaults to one catch-all entry listing @samp{~/repos/*}.

Each source is either a registry file, whose name ends in @samp{.json}, or a path pattern.

A path pattern may contain wildcards. A wildcard is @samp{*}, @samp{?}, or a @samp{[...]} character class, and a pattern containing one is expanded a single level per wildcard component: @samp{~/repos/*} reaches the children of @samp{~/repos/}, and @samp{~/repos/.worktrees/*/*} its grandchildren two levels down. Wildcard expansion keeps only git repositories and worktrees; a worktree records its git directory in a file rather than a directory, and both count. A pattern without a wildcard names exactly one directory, which is kept whether or not it is a repository, and dropped when it does not exist or is not a directory. A pattern that cannot be expanded at all, such as one holding an unbalanced @samp{[}, is reported in the echo area and skipped, so one malformed source never suppresses the candidates of the others.

A registry is a JSON file holding a @samp{projects} array, in the format the obsolete @code{agent-epoch-project-registry-file} used to read (@ref{Shared session behavior}). Every entry becomes one candidate, labelled by its @samp{id} and @samp{title}, or by its @samp{id} alone when it records no title. Unlike a path pattern it carries a description: the entry's @samp{summary}, or its @samp{aliases} when it records no summary, followed by its @samp{browser_keywords} and its Slack channels; an entry recording none of the four has no description at all rather than an empty one. The keywords are worth their length: they are the terms the project is recognized by elsewhere, repository slugs and domain names among them, which is what a routed Slack message or email is likeliest to name, whereas aliases are often the title restated. An entry that records a JSON @samp{null} where a string belongs counts as recording nothing, so a null summary falls back to the aliases just as an omitted one does. Later stages use that text to judge which project a piece of text is about. An entry's directory is its first @samp{project_doc_paths} folder when it records one, because that folder holds the project's instruction files, its first @samp{repo_paths} directory when it records only that, and the registry root itself when it records neither. The user option @code{agent-project-registry-root} names the directory those relative paths are expanded against; it defaults to @code{nil}, and a registry recording relative paths is unusable until it is set. A source naming a registry that does not exist raises an error rather than being skipped, since it points at a file the user meant to have.

The function @code{agent-project-candidates} returns the candidates for an account as plists with @code{:label}, @code{:directory}, @code{:repository} and @code{:description}. The repository is the candidate's local clone: the directory itself for a path source that is a git repository or worktree, the first @samp{repo_paths} directory for a registry entry, and nil for a project holding no clone of its own. It is what @code{agent-project-repository-directory} answers from, which returns the clone of a named repository for an account, or nil when no source holds one that exists. Forge routing asks it for a repository whose worktree Forge does not know or no longer knows, which is what a repository that moved looks like (@ref{Shared commands}). Candidates are deduplicated by true path, so listing the same directory twice under different names, or through a symbolic link, yields one entry. A label shared by two candidates is replaced with the abbreviated directory, which is what then appears in completion, so no two projects offer the same string.

The function @code{agent-project-read} asks for one of those candidates. It takes the account, the text of the thing being routed, the completion prompt, a callback and an optional suggested directory, and calls the callback with the directory of the project chosen. A suggestion is offered first, which makes it what a bare @samp{RET} answers, and it joins the candidates when no source holds it, so a caller that worked its directory out for itself leads the choice instead of bypassing it. Nothing is ranked when a suggestion is given, since a caller that already knows the project has no use for a model's opinion of it. It answers through the callback even when nothing is ranked, so every caller reads the answer the same way: the directory arrives in the callback, never as a return value. An account whose sources contribute no candidate at all raises an error rather than offering an empty prompt.

The candidates are ordered by a model before completion only when there is something to order them by: the routed text must hold more than whitespace, and at least one candidate must carry a description holding more than whitespace. A blank string counts as no text on either side, so candidates that came from path patterns alone, or from registry entries whose summary, aliases, keywords and Slack channels are all empty, go straight to completion and cost no request. An empty summary alone is not enough: the keywords and channels are appended to it, so an entry recording a blank summary and one keyword still carries text and is still ranked. When the request is made, @code{agent-project-ranking-model} and @code{agent-project-ranking-backend} name the model and the @samp{gptel} backend it runs on; they default to @code{gemini-flash-lite-latest} on the @samp{"Gemini"} backend, and ranking requires @samp{gptel}, which is otherwise an optional dependency. The model is asked for the candidate labels, comma separated, most likely first. A name it returns is matched to the label it equals, and failing that to the shortest label it starts, so a name the model shortened still finds its project while a label that is itself the start of another still names its own. The candidates it names move to the front of the completion list in the order it gave them, each appearing once however often it is named, and the rest keep their original order behind them, so an answer that names only some projects, repeats one, or invents a label never hides a project. A request that fails is reported in the echo area and leaves the original order standing, so a ranking failure costs order rather than the whole action.

@node Org TODO workflows
@section Org TODO workflows

@vindex agent-todo-log-directory
@vindex agent-todo-in-progress-keyword
The @samp{agent-todo} module has two options, both formerly named after Claude. @code{agent-todo-log-directory} names the directory under which @code{agent-batch-todos} writes one timestamped run directory per batch, holding the raw backend output for each entry; it replaces @code{agent-claude-log-directory}. @code{agent-todo-in-progress-keyword} is the Org TODO keyword @code{agent-send-todo-at-point} moves a heading to once it has been sent, and it replaces @code{agent-claude-org-todo-in-progress-keyword}. Both old names remain as obsolete aliases. The keyword defaults to @code{nil}, which leaves the TODO state alone, because Org ships with only TODO and DONE and has no intermediate state to move to; set it to whichever in-progress keyword your own @code{org-todo-keywords} declares.

@node Claude Code backend
@section Claude Code backend

@vindex agent-claude-status-interval
@vindex agent-claude-interrupt-check-delay
@vindex agent-claude-submit-delay
@vindex agent-claude-submit-retries
@vindex agent-claude-accounts
