@findex agent-debug-backtrace
@findex agent-act-on-slack-message
@findex agent-act-on-forge-notification
@findex agent-act-on-email
@findex agent-exit
@findex agent-exit-without-skills
@findex agent-restart
@findex agent-history
@findex agent-setup-kill-on-exit
@findex agent-setup-scroll-keys
@findex agent-snippet-tab
The command @code{agent-menu} opens the unified transient menu. Every entry in it works with either backend: session switching, waiting-session jumps, resuming a past session, creating a branch of the current session, switching between branches, handoff, restart, exit, history browsing, skill execution, project audit, one dispatcher that acts on the thing at point, and a Prompts section beneath Tools in the same column for prompt capture, captured prompt insertion, and batch TODO processing.

The Sessions section holds general session management: new session (@samp{n}),
resume session (@samp{r}), select session (@samp{s}), jump to waiting, and open log.
The Account section beneath Options holds login (@samp{a l}) and usage (@samp{a u}).
The Current session column holds branching, toggle snooze (@samp{z}), handoff,
restart (@samp{R}), and exit commands. It labels
@code{agent-exit} (@samp{x}) as “exit” and @code{agent-exit-without-skills} (@samp{X}) as “exit immediately”.

Tools use the @samp{t} prefix: act on thing at point (@samp{t .}), run skill (@samp{t r}),
audit project (@samp{t a}), post-push CI (@samp{t c}), and orchestration run (@samp{t o}).
Prompts use @samp{p}: capture (@samp{p c}), insert (@samp{p i}), and batch TODOs (@samp{p b}).
Options put account selection first, then buffer protection and branch warnings,
followed by ready alerts and theme synchronization.

@findex agent-history
The command @code{agent-history}, labeled “open log” and bound to @samp{l} in the Sessions column, opens the Agent Log menu for browsing, opening, searching, and resuming historical sessions across backends. Agent Log is an optional package: loading @samp{agent} never requires it, @code{agent-history} loads it on demand, and invoking the command without Agent Log installed signals a clear error. Neither @code{agent-menu} nor the session switcher reads any historical catalog while opening; catalog work (which for Codex can start an app-server process and take seconds) happens only after an explicit Agent Log action.

In EAT-backed Claude and Codex session buffers, @code{agent-setup-scroll-keys} makes PageUp and PageDown send terminal mouse-wheel events, matching the TUI scroll path used by the mouse. PageUp and PageDown in other selected buffers retain their normal bindings and never target a visible AI terminal in another window. Terminal helpers require buffer-local EAT state, so native app-server sessions cannot inherit a stale terminal from a killed buffer.

The command @code{agent-select-session} opens the session switcher, which contains only existing session entries. If no sessions are active, it reports that there are none; use @code{agent-start-new-session} (@samp{n}) to start one. Sessions receive stable home-row keys where possible and display backend icons next to their labels.

The switcher lists every session in one flat list ordered by home-row key, whatever account each session runs under. The account is a column of its own, between the session name and its annotation, shown dimmed in the @code{agent-session-account} face. A session is therefore found at the key it always answers to, rather than at a position that moves when a neighbor's account changes. Sessions whose backend has no accounts configured leave the column blank, and when no session on screen records an account the column is left out altogether.

A session that is waiting for something you can only do later can be set aside with @code{agent-toggle-snooze}, bound to @samp{z} in @code{agent-menu}; invoking it again unsnoozes. A snoozed session keeps its key and its buffer, but the switcher lists it after every active session in the dimmed italic @code{agent-snoozed} face instead of a state color, @code{agent-jump-to-waiting} skips it, and it fires no ready alert when its turn ends. When snoozing, the command reads a duration in hours from the minibuffer; leaving it blank snoozes indefinitely. A timed snooze ends by itself when the hours run out, and if the session is waiting for input at that point, the ready alert that snoozing suppressed fires then. The command acts on the current session, and prompts for one when the current buffer is not a session or with a prefix argument. The mark is a property of the buffer, so it ends with the session and is not carried over by a restart or a handoff.

@vindex agent-session-annotation-functions
Sessions can also carry a one-line annotation after their name, shown dimmed in the @code{agent-session-annotation} face. The text comes from @code{agent-session-annotation-functions}, an abnormal hook: each function is called with a session buffer and returns a string, or @code{nil} to leave the session to the functions after it. The first non-nil answer is the one shown, and the functions after it are not called. @samp{agent} renders whatever it returns and knows nothing about where the text comes from. Whitespace in the answer is collapsed to single spaces, so a multi-line answer cannot break the menu's layout, and an answer that is blank or not a string counts as no annotation. Sessions without an annotation — including sessions every function declines to annotate — render exactly as they did before annotations existed. The default is @code{nil}, which shows session names alone; Agent Log adds a function that supplies each session's stored one-line summary (@uref{https://github.com/benthamite/agent-log, agent-log}), and with no such integration loaded the switcher looks unchanged. Because this is a hook, an integration adds itself with @code{add-hook} rather than taking a slot over: a function you installed yourself keeps working whichever loads first, and @code{remove-hook} takes either one back out.

@vindex agent-session-state-summary-functions
@vindex agent-session-status-token-functions
@findex agent-session-set-status
The switcher keeps the session summary and adds two columns after it: the
age of the current state and a short state description published by the agent.
Ages use seconds, minutes, hours, or days (@samp{12s}, @samp{2m}, @samp{1h}, @samp{3d}), measured
from the last observed state transition. Repeated events reporting the same
state do not reset the age; neither do changes to either summary. An
unobserved transition has no age. Ages refresh every five seconds while the
menu is open.

The state description is explicitly authored by the working agent, for
example “Testing pagination fix” or “Waiting for your decision about bindings”.
It is not extracted from the chat and requires no separate summarizer.
Until an agent publishes an update, the column is blank. A new turn or a
changed native session identity clears the previous description; a completed
turn retains its final update. Publication does not alter the state color or
its age, and a claim of completion remains the agent's report.

The executable @samp{bin/agent-status} accepts a status string of at most 160
characters (an empty string clears it). It routes through @samp{CODEX_BUFFER_NAME}
or @samp{CLAUDE_BUFFER_NAME}, requiring @samp{--backend codex} or
@samp{--backend claude-code} if both are present. Codex requires @samp{CODEX_THREAD_ID} to match the backend's current native
identity, including before the first submission has cached that identity.
A registered identity resolver is authoritative even when it returns no id;
only backends without one use their recorded identity. Claude requires @samp{AGENT_SESSION_UUID} to match its process token,
so an old process cannot update a replacement session with the same name. The helper
rejects absent or mismatched sessions, passes text as encoded JSON rather
than executable Lisp, and reports failures. @samp{--socket-name} (or
@samp{EMACS_SOCKET_NAME}) selects a non-default Emacs server. Only the main agent
should publish status: subagents can inherit their parent's buffer variable.

Instruct agents to publish a factual phrase of roughly 5–12 words at task
start, meaningful phase changes, blockers, and before their final response.
Installing this instruction affects sessions that read it; already running
agents need to receive the instruction before they will publish updates.

The public function @code{agent-session-set-status} accepts a buffer name, backend
symbol, status text, optional native session id, and optional process token. The hook @code{agent-session-status-token-functions} supplies backend process tokens.
The function validates the target and text, stores the update in that buffer, and refreshes an open switcher.
@code{agent-session-state-summary-functions} supplies the column using the same
cheap, first-non-nil convention as the session-summary hook. The built-in
provider reads the published status without transcript I/O or model calls.

Names, accounts, and annotations each line up in one column: names are padded to the widest name in the menu and accounts to the widest account, measured in display columns, so a name containing double-width characters does not push the columns after it to the right. Padding that nothing follows is trimmed, so a session with no annotation ends at its account, and a session with neither ends at its name.

@vindex agent-session-annotation-max-width
The session summary and state-description columns are aligned across rows and
truncated with an ellipsis to fit the frame. After reserving the name,
account, graphical icon width, age, separators, and menu margins, each text
column can use half
the remaining width; space unused by a short update is available to the
session summary, and space unused by a short summary is available to the
update. @code{agent-session-annotation-max-width} caps only the session summary.
A zero cap hides that summary without hiding the age or state description.
When neither new column has data, the original annotation layout is used.


A function added to @code{agent-session-annotation-functions} must be cheap and free of side effects: building the switcher calls it more than once per session, because aligning the annotations needs every label's width before any label is final. Reading a value from memory is appropriate; reading a file or contacting a process is not.

The command @code{agent-run-skill} discovers skills from every loaded backend that implements skill discovery. It disambiguates same-named skills by backend label, prompts for declared arguments when a skill frontmatter requests them, and dispatches the invocation to the selected backend. Claude and Codex backends scan global and project-local programmatic skill directories, using both @code{project-current} and nearby tool directories as project-root signals, so batch-only skills can be run from this command without being loaded into ordinary interactive agent sessions.

The command @code{agent-capture-prompt} opens a session-specific Org file and appends a new prompt entry. The command @code{agent-insert-captured-prompt} reads pending nonempty entries for the current session, prompts for one with a short body preview in the completion candidate, inserts its text into the backend prompt without submitting it, and removes the selected Org entry from the capture file. With a prefix argument, it includes legacy entries already marked as inserted. When a session still has pending captured prompts, @code{agent-exit}, @code{agent-exit-without-skills}, @code{agent-restart}, and @code{agent-handoff} ask for confirmation before closing or replacing that session.

The command @code{agent-post-push-ci} runs the @code{post-push-ci} skill through the
selected backend with @samp{--no-push --commit HEAD}. Use it after a push has
already happened and you want an agent to inspect GitHub Actions failures, fix
the root cause, and continue the authorized CI closeout loop.

The command @code{agent-act-on-slack-message}, called on a message in a Slack room
buffer, starts a session in the project that message is about and inserts its
permalink into the prompt without submitting it. With a prefix argument it
inserts the permalink into a running session instead. Its extractor,
@code{agent-slack-context}, answers with the message text and no directory, since a
message names no project of its own: the project is read from the account's
sources and ranked by that text (@ref{Project sources}).

The command @code{agent-act-on-forge-notification} is the Forge counterpart of
@code{agent-act-on-slack-message}. Called on a notification in
@code{forge-list-notifications}, it starts a session in the working tree of the
repository that notification belongs to and inserts the issue or pull request
URL into the prompt without submitting it. It also accepts the topic at point,
so it works from a topic buffer or a Magit status buffer, and with a prefix
argument it sends the URL to a running session instead of starting one. Not
every notification is about a topic: Github also notifies about check suites,
commits and releases, and Forge records no topic for those. Such a notification
is routed as itself, because it names a repository to work in and a URL to
read; its title goes into the prompt with the URL, since the URL of a failed
workflow run names the page listing every run of the repository and the title
is what says which one this is. The
command is a thin wrapper over the routing layer described below: its
extractor, @code{agent-forge-context}, reads the subject at point and answers with a
context suggesting the working tree, so the project reader opens on that clone
already selected: a bare @samp{RET} starts there, and the same prompt is where a
different project is chosen instead. Routing a topic therefore ends in the same
choice as routing a Slack message or a backtrace, rather than starting a session
outright.

A Slack message has to be classified to find its project, whereas a Forge
notification already records its repository. Where that repository is cloned is
a second question, and it is asked in three steps. Forge answers first, from the
worktree it recorded; it records one only for a repository it has seen from a
local clone, and it forgets one whose path stopped being a worktree, which is
what moving a repository does to every path at once. The account's project
sources answer next, with @code{agent-project-repository-directory}: they are where
the clones live, so they know the current path of a repository that moved and
the path of one Forge never saw. When neither knows, the context carries no
directory and the core reads a project from its text, which leads with the
repository's slug so that ranking puts the right project first. Nothing is
refused for want of a clone.

The command @code{agent-act-on-email}, called on a message in a mu4e headers or
view buffer, starts a session in the project that message is about and inserts
the message's maildir path into the prompt without submitting it. With a
prefix argument it sends the path to a running session instead. The payload is
the path rather than the text of the mail because an agent can open that file
itself and read the headers, the body and any attachments from it, which no
excerpt pasted into a prompt would carry. Like Slack routing and unlike Forge
routing, the context names no directory: a message belongs to no project of its
own, so its extractor, @code{agent-mu4e-context}, answers with the subject and the
sender as the text the project is ranked against (@ref{Project sources}).

The sender comes from the message's @samp{:from} field, which @samp{mu} returns as a
list of @samp{(:email EMAIL :name NAME)} plists — one per sender, with the name
absent when the sender gave none — and reads as @samp{Name <address>}, or as the
bare address when there is no name. Like its Slack and Forge counterparts, the
command loads its foreign package itself and says so when it is missing, since
the command is autoloaded and can therefore be called from a buffer that has
never loaded mu4e.

It signals an error when the message records no path — mu4e reports a missing
string field as the empty string rather than as nil, so an empty path counts as
no path — and when the recorded file cannot be read. A maildir file name
encodes the message's flags, so reading a message, or another mail program
touching it, renames the file, and mu's index can name a path that no longer
exists until it is reindexed. As with a stale Forge clone path, that is an
ordinary failure, and catching it here is better than handing the agent a path
it cannot open.

@findex agent-act-on-thing-at-point
@vindex agent-at-point-things
The menu binds no routing command directly. @code{agent-act-on-thing-at-point}, bound to @samp{t .} in the Tools column, walks @code{agent-at-point-things} and runs the extractor of the first entry whose predicate matches the current buffer: a backtrace in a @samp{*Backtrace*} buffer, a Slack message in a Slack room buffer, a Forge notification or topic, an email in a mu4e headers or view buffer, or an org TODO heading. Every predicate answers from the buffer alone — the buffer name, the buffer-local variable @samp{slack} sets on room buffers, the major mode, the org TODO state — so dispatch never loads a module to ask whether it applies, and the Forge and mu4e lookups run only in a buffer whose major mode can hold what they look for. A Magit buffer is one of those, and it can be current with @samp{forge} not yet loaded; since forge's lookups are not autoloaded, the Forge predicate also checks that they are defined, and falls through to the next thing rather than failing when they are not. The predicate claims any notification, not only one about a topic, because the extractor routes the notification itself when Forge records no topic for it. The mu4e modes need no such check, because the files that define those modes require the one that defines the lookup. When nothing matches, the command signals an error naming those five kinds, in the order the table holds them.

Recognizing the thing, choosing the session, and delivering the payload are three separate layers. An extractor takes a continuation rather than returning a value, because extraction can require a network round-trip, and calls it with a context plist: @samp{:directory} when the thing carries a working directory, as an absolute path, @samp{:suggested-directory} when it worked one out and the user should still be asked, @samp{:text} when it carries only text to choose a project from, @samp{:payload} for what to send, @samp{:submit} for whether to submit it, and an optional @samp{:after} thunk run once the payload is delivered. Before extraction begins, the core resolves the backend and captures that backend's selected account as one pair. A later account switch cannot change which project sources this invocation reads, which matters for asynchronous extractors such as Slack. A context that names a directory starts its session there; one that suggests a directory, or names none at all, has its project read from the captured account's sources with @code{agent-project-read}, led by the suggestion or ranked by the text. With a prefix argument the payload goes to a running session chosen by completion instead of a new one, so the same key routes a thing either way.

A running session already has a directory, so on that path the core tells the extractor not to resolve one, and an extractor that has to work for its directory leaves it out. That saves what the work costs: routing a backtrace to a running session asks no model which packages it implicates and prompts for none, and routing a Forge topic there no longer refuses a repository that was never cloned locally. The extractors that carry only text are unaffected, since the project they would otherwise pick is chosen after the session is, and only for a new one.

What each kind sends differs, because the payloads differ. A Slack permalink, a Forge topic URL and an email's maildir path are references to something the agent should read, so they arrive unsubmitted and you type the request next to them. A backtrace and an org TODO already are the request, so they submit.

A payload marked @samp{:submit} reaches a @strong{new} session as its initial prompt, handed to the backend at launch rather than typed into the terminal afterwards. Claude passes it on the CLI's command line, and Codex does too except when it is resuming a session or running through its app server, where it sends the prompt itself once the session is up. Either way the payload never races the terminal: nothing sequences typed input against a session whose process has only just been spawned, and these sessions often start in a directory the CLI has never opened, where it greets the user with a trust prompt that the typed text would answer instead of the model. A payload the context does not submit is typed in on both paths, since it is meant to sit in the prompt for the user to edit. A submitted payload reaching an already-running session is typed in and then submitted, because that session is past its startup and needs no hand-off at launch.

Because extraction can be asynchronous, the continuation may run long after the command was invoked, in whatever buffer the user has moved to since. The dispatcher therefore captures the invocation buffer and resolves the backend up front, before the extractor runs: the session adopts the backend of the buffer the thing was read from rather than of wherever the callback lands, no backend prompt appears in the middle of an asynchronous wait, and the @samp{:after} thunk runs back in the invocation buffer, which starting a session has displaced as the current one. A thunk whose buffer has been killed meanwhile is dropped, since the thing it would act on is gone.

The commands @code{agent-handoff}, @code{agent-audit-project}, @code{agent-debug-backtrace}, @code{agent-act-on-slack-message}, @code{agent-exit}, and @code{agent-restart} resolve a backend from the current session buffer or by prompting. This lets the same keybinding operate on Claude or Codex sessions without separate wrapper commands. The old per-backend command names (@code{agent-claude-handoff}, @code{agent-codex-restart}, and so on) remain available as obsolete aliases. @code{agent-exit}, bound to @samp{x}, kills the session buffer after any configured before-exit skills finish. @code{agent-exit-without-skills}, bound to @samp{X}, uses the same exit path but inhibits the configured before-exit skills. It still checks for pending captured prompts and runs other before-exit functions. Neither command deletes the Emacs window that displayed the session.

@code{agent-handoff} reads the backend's entry in @code{agent-handoff-files}, kills the current session buffer, and starts a new session with the handoff prompt. By default the new session keeps the source directory. A handoff file can select another directory with YAML front matter:

@example
---
target-directory: ../other-project
---

Continue from this handoff.
@end example

The target can be absolute or relative to the source session directory, and it must name an existing directory. @code{agent-handoff} resolves and validates it before it kills the source session. Lisp callers can pass the optional @code{TARGET-DIRECTORY} argument instead; an explicit argument takes precedence over file metadata, and file metadata takes precedence over the source directory.

@code{agent-handoff-from-emacsclient} accepts the source buffer name as its first @samp{emacsclient} argument and the optional target directory as its second argument. This lets agent-authored handoff commands close the requesting session even though Emacs evaluates the form outside that buffer. If the source buffer name is unavailable, handoff closes the single existing session buffer for the source directory before starting the replacement session, which avoids the CLI's instance-name prompt in unattended loops.

@code{agent-restart} kills the current session buffer and resumes the same session in place, preserving the session's directory, instance name, and any launch state the backend's @code{:restart-options} function captures. It reroutes sessions within the selected account pool without prompting. Otherwise it prompts when the active account differs from the session account, and it leaves the account unset when neither the session nor the global selection names one, so a session that ran under the backend's ambient default home is restarted there rather than pushed onto an account it never used.  Every precondition is checked before the old session is killed: restart refuses when a named account is no longer configured, when the account is logged out, or when no resumable session identity exists, and in each case the running session survives untouched. @code{agent-audit-project} prompts for a project directory and runs each skill in @code{agent-audit-skills} sequentially through the backend's non-interactive prompt runner. @code{agent-debug-backtrace} saves the current Emacs backtrace, asks @samp{gptel} to identify likely packages, and submits instructions to fix the bug to a session started in the selected package source directory, or to a running session under a prefix argument. Its extractor, @code{agent--backtrace-context}, schedules the identification to run after the current command rather than doing it inline, because saving the backtrace kills the @samp{*Backtrace*} buffer, which exits the debugger's recursive edit and unwinds the calling frame. @code{agent-setup-kill-on-exit} arranges for a session buffer to be killed when its process exits, consulting the backend's @code{:before-kill-check} slot first. It is not a menu entry: each backend installs it, along with @code{agent-disable-scrollback-truncation} and, for Codex, @code{agent-fix-rendering}, from its session-start hook, so the menu carries no buffer-maintenance commands.

@code{agent-before-exit-functions} can delay @code{agent-exit} for cleanup work.
Each function receives the resolved backend and session buffer; returning nil
aborts the exit.
When a turn is active, Agent waits for it to finish before it submits the first
before-exit skill, so the skill cannot steer or abort the user's turn.  A
backend that reports both a stop and a later idle-prompt notification for one
turn advances the chain only once.
Once a forced session close proceeds, Agent kills the target buffer without
running buffer-local kill-query prompts, so stale session buffers do not survive
restart or exit workflows.

Set @code{agent-before-exit-skill-name} (a single skill) or
@code{agent-before-exit-skill-names} (an ordered list) to submit interactive skills
before exiting matching sessions. Each entry in @code{agent-before-exit-skill-names}
is either a skill-name string or a list whose car is the skill name and whose
cdr is a plist accepting @code{:directories} (restrict where that skill runs,
overriding @code{agent-before-exit-skill-directories}) and @code{:args} (a string
appended to the submitted command). Set @code{:receipt} non-nil when the skill must
confirm its closeout result before the chain can continue. For example:

@lisp
(setq agent-before-exit-skill-names
      '("session-learning-capture"
        ("update-log" :directories ("~/My Drive/Epoch")
                      :args "--auto" :receipt t)))
@end lisp

@code{agent-run-skill-before-exit} uses each backend's registered skill command
prefix so Claude sessions receive slash commands and Codex sessions receive
dollar-prefixed skill commands, then submits the first applicable skill. The
skills run in order, each submitted after the previous one finishes, and the
session exits after the last. These submissions do not display the session, so
an exit chain continues in the background after the user switches away;
ordinary ready notifications are left unchanged while the chain runs. When
@code{agent-before-exit-skill-names} is nil, @code{agent-before-exit-skill-name} is used
as a single-skill fallback. Leave @code{agent-before-exit-skill-directories} nil to
run an unscoped skill before exiting every session.

For a @code{:receipt} entry, Agent creates a private temporary directory and appends
@code{--receipt-file PATH} to the submitted command. The skill must write one JSON
object to that path. Its @code{status} must be exactly @code{success}, @code{no-op}, or
@code{failure}; a failure receipt can include a @code{message}. Agent advances the chain
or exits only after @code{success} or @code{no-op}. A missing file, invalid JSON, unknown
status, or @code{failure} stops the chain, keeps the session open, and shows a
warning; a missing file is ignored while the backend's idle probe still shows
the session working, since that completion event belongs to an earlier turn. Agent removes the temporary receipt directory after reading it or
tearing down the session. Receipt gating is per entry: plain entries keep the
stop-event behavior. This lets a learning-capture entry run independently
before a receipt-gated bookkeeping entry. Repeating @code{agent-exit} while a chain
is active does not bypass it; it reports the chain's state in the echo area,
and @code{agent-exit-without-skills} is the explicit close that skips configured
skills. When @code{agent-exit} is called on a session that is still responding, the
chain arms in a waiting state and starts its first skill on the next stop
event; a message says so at arming time, since nothing else is visible until
the turn ends.

@code{agent-exit} runs every applicable configured skill regardless of session
duration. @code{agent-exit-without-skills} is the only exit command that bypasses
the skill hook; it still runs other before-exit functions.

The chain is driven by session stop events and guarded by a watchdog. Each
submitted skill gets up to @code{agent-before-exit-timeout} seconds (default 600) to
finish; the watchdog restarts when the next skill begins. If the current skill
exceeds that interval, the watchdog warns but keeps the exit request armed. The
chain checks the result when the skill eventually finishes instead of losing
the requested exit because the skill ran for more than ten minutes. A gated
entry still needs an accepted receipt at that point.

Backends can veto that automatic close while the submitted skill invocation is
still visible as unaccepted prompt input. Codex uses this to avoid closing a
session when the dollar-prefixed skill name was inserted but not submitted,
while ignoring submitted command scrollback, visible placeholder text, and
autosuggestion text. The readiness check recognises the standard Codex prompt
markers (@samp{›} and @samp{❯}); a chain that stalls after the first skill usually means
the running Codex renders an unrecognised prompt marker.

A chain stays armed across stop events until its queue drains, so a chain
whose command was typed but never accepted would otherwise fire its next skill
into whatever the user does in that buffer later. A prompt the user sends from
the terminal therefore cancels any armed chain in that buffer, with a message.
Claude reports such prompts as a @code{user-submit} session event from its Return
key paths; Codex's submission hook runs for programmatic sends too, so it
reports plain @code{submit} events and cannot cancel a chain this way.

Backends can provide an atomic submit operation for before-exit commands.
Claude and Codex both target the resolved session buffer directly, insert the
skill command, and submit it before the terminal buffer is displayed again.
Codex delegates this to @samp{codex.el}'s target-buffer submit helper. Claude
inserts the command, waits @code{agent-claude-submit-delay} seconds with
@code{sleep-for} (pending keyboard input cannot shorten it, as it would a
@code{sit-for}, and a Return that arrives too soon is folded into the inserted text
as a newline), and sends Return. It then checks the prompt line and re-sends
Return up to @code{agent-claude-submit-retries} times while the command is still
sitting there unsubmitted, since Claude Code occasionally swallows the first
one; the prompt marker's trailing space is a no-break space under @samp{eat}.

@node Orchestration run view
@section Orchestration run view

@findex agent-run-open
@findex agent-run-mode
The command @code{agent-run-open}, bound to @samp{t o} in the Tools column of
@code{agent-menu}, opens an @samp{orchestrate-review} run JSON file in a read-only
@code{agent-run-mode} buffer. Select the private run file created by the skill;
the command remembers opened filenames for the current Emacs session and
does not scan temporary directories. Each run has its own view.

The view shows the recorded stage, phase, status, author and reviewer, and
the latest stage-level progress with the progress file's age. Pending delivery
reconciliation and a recorded incomplete return appear as attention items.
Old progress does not establish that an actor stopped. A returned implementation
is distinct from a completed run; the helper still owns verification evidence
and completion. This view does not validate the full workflow contract.

Session links recheck the recorded native identity before opening a buffer.
An unavailable or replaced session is labeled unavailable. Legacy version-2
runs remain readable, but actors without recorded identities cannot be linked.
File links open the run record, progress file, run directory, or repository.
The run format does not record ordinary spec/plan paths or blocker descriptions;
the view says so rather than looking through conversations to infer them.

Visible views refresh every five seconds; @samp{g} refreshes immediately and @samp{q}
quits the window. Killing a view cancels its refresh timer. A read failure
replaces the display with an explicit error, so stale data cannot appear current.
Only local files are supported. Run reads are limited to 4 MiB, and progress
reads to the final 16 KiB@. The view never submits prompts, advances phases,
reads transcripts, inspects internal tasks, or starts workers.

@node Branch navigation
@section Branch navigation

@findex agent-resume
@findex agent-create-branch
@findex agent-switch-branch
A branch is a session forked from another one: it starts with the parent's whole conversation and then diverges. Branching lets you try a different approach without losing the state you built up, and the three commands here work the same way whichever backend the session belongs to.

@code{agent-create-branch}, bound to @samp{b n}, forks the session in the current buffer and opens the fork in its own buffer under an instance name derived from the time of the fork. By default the branch shares the parent's working tree, which is what starting a second session in the same project would give you. With a prefix argument it also creates a git worktree on a fresh branch under @code{agent-branch-worktree-directory} and runs the fork there, so that destructive git operations in one branch cannot disturb the other. The worktree starts at the parent's HEAD, so uncommitted parent changes are not carried into it; use the isolated form when concurrent git work across branches is a real concern, and the default otherwise. Before the fork starts, the backend's @code{:prepare-fork} slot makes the parent session visible from the new directory, which is what a backend that stores its transcripts per project directory needs in order to resume a session recorded elsewhere.

@code{agent-switch-branch}, bound to @samp{b s}, shows every session sharing a fork ancestor with the current one as an indented tree, each entry carrying its first prompt and its timestamp and the session you are in marked with an asterisk, and switches to the selected session: to its live buffer when it still has one, and otherwise by resuming it in a new buffer. It reports that there is nothing to navigate when the current session has no relatives.

@code{agent-resume}, bound to @samp{R}, resumes a past session of the current backend, or of a backend it asks for when no session buffer names one, and passes its prefix argument through to the backend, where it means the most recent session.

Both backends supply the lineage these commands read, through the @code{:session-headers} slot, and each derives it from what its CLI already records. Claude Code writes one transcript per session inside a per-project directory, so the scan reads the first line of each transcript in the project the current session belongs to. Codex writes one rollout file per session, all under a single sessions directory, so the scan reads the @code{session_meta} record opening each rollout, keeps the ones whose recorded working directory matches, and takes the parent from @code{forked_from_id}. Because that directory holds every Codex session on the machine, the scan is bounded by a start timestamp when the caller only needs one session's descendants.

Codex records subagent threads with the same @code{forked_from_id} field, and those are deliberately left out of the tree. They are forks as far as the file format is concerned, but they are not branches of your conversation: they are threads the model spawned to do work on its own, and offering to switch into one would be offering something a user never chose to create.

@node Org TODO commands
@section Org TODO commands

@findex agent-batch-todos
@findex agent-send-todo-at-point
The @samp{agent-todo} module turns Org TODO entries into work for an AI session, and both of its commands work with either backend.

@code{agent-send-todo-at-point} formats the heading and body of the TODO at point as a prompt and submits it to a session started for it; it is not a menu entry of its own, since the menu reaches the same routing through @code{agent-act-on-thing-at-point}. With a prefix argument it submits the prompt to a running session chosen by completion. Like a Slack message, a TODO names no directory, so the project is read from the account's sources and ranked by the TODO's own text (@ref{Project sources}). It no longer looks for the session belonging to the current file's project: an Org file of tasks rarely sits in the repository those tasks are about. When @code{agent-todo-in-progress-keyword} is set, the heading moves to that keyword once the prompt has been delivered. The heading is remembered as a marker, so the move lands on the heading the prompt came from however far point has travelled while the project was being chosen. A heading deleted in the meantime is left alone: the state change happens after the session exists but before it is shown, so an error there would leave a running session unshown.

@code{agent-batch-todos}, bound to @samp{p b}, collects several TODO entries and runs them one after another without an interactive session. It infers its scope by itself — the region when one is active, the subtree when the buffer is narrowed, and the whole buffer otherwise — then asks for a working directory, and confirms the number of entries before starting unless you selected them as a region, where the selection is confirmation enough. Each entry runs through the resolved backend's @code{:exec-prompt} slot, its raw output is written to a numbered file under a timestamped directory in @code{agent-todo-log-directory}, and a summary buffer collects every entry's status, duration, cost, and output when the queue is empty.

Cost appears per entry only for backends whose CLI reports it. Claude's non-interactive runs report a dollar figure and it is shown; @samp{codex exec} reports none, so Codex entries render an em dash in place of a cost and contribute nothing to the total.

@node Chief-of-staff commands
@section Chief-of-staff commands

@findex agent-chief-start
@findex agent-chief-start-session
@findex agent-chief-stop
@findex agent-chief-tick
@findex agent-chief-stateless-tick
@findex agent-chief-session-heartbeat
@findex agent-chief-switch-to-session
@findex agent-chief-open-state
@findex agent-chief-add-note
@findex agent-chief-set-day-plan
The command @code{agent-chief-start-session} starts or reuses a dedicated Claude Code or Codex session using @code{agent-chief-session-instance-name}, sends the chief-of-staff operating contract into the session, asks the model to request today's plan there, and starts the heartbeat timer. The command @code{agent-chief-switch-to-session} jumps back to that session so the user can reply normally.

The command @code{agent-chief-start} starts only the repeating Emacs timer. Without a prefix argument it also schedules an immediate tick; with a prefix argument, it waits @code{agent-chief-interval} seconds before the first tick. @code{agent-chief-stop} cancels the timer. @code{agent-chief-tick} runs one check using @code{agent-chief-mode}. @code{agent-chief-session-heartbeat} forces a session-mode heartbeat, while @code{agent-chief-stateless-tick} forces one stateless JSON-decision check.

The command @code{agent-chief-open-state} opens the auditable Org state file. @code{agent-chief-add-note} appends a manual note, and @code{agent-chief-set-day-plan} appends today's plan. When a chief session is active, both commands also submit the new context into that session. These commands are intentionally simple: the durable source of truth remains ordinary text that can be reviewed, edited, and audited outside the model.

@node Claude Code commands
@section Claude Code commands

@findex agent-claude-start-or-switch
@findex agent-claude-select-account
@findex agent-claude-init-account
@findex agent-claude-start-status-polling
@findex agent-claude-stop-status-polling
@findex agent-claude-ensure-statusline-config
@findex agent-claude-ensure-stop-hook-config
@findex agent-claude-ensure-notification-hook-config
@findex agent-claude-ensure-state-hook-config
@findex agent-claude-setup-config
The Claude commands expose workflows that are specific to Claude Code; handoff, restart, exit, resume, branch navigation, skill execution, project audits, backtrace debugging, TODO workflows, and Slack-message routing use the unified @code{agent-*} commands described above. @code{agent-claude-start-or-switch} starts Claude under the selected account or opens the unified switcher. @code{agent-claude-select-account} persists an account choice, and @code{agent-claude-init-account} prepares a new account directory with shared configuration files and symlinks.

The branch and TODO commands that used to be Claude's own are now the shared @code{agent-create-branch}, @code{agent-switch-branch}, @code{agent-batch-todos}, and @code{agent-send-todo-at-point}; @code{agent-claude-create-branch}, @code{agent-claude-switch-branch}, @code{agent-claude-batch-todos}, and @code{agent-claude-send-todo-at-point} remain as obsolete aliases.

@code{agent-claude-start-status-polling} and @code{agent-claude-stop-status-polling} start and stop status polling for the current Claude buffer by hand. Polling is started and stopped for you with the session, so these are maintenance commands rather than part of a normal workflow; they are available through @samp{M-x} and are not in @code{agent-menu}.

The setup command @code{agent-claude-setup-config} ensures that Claude's statusline, Stop hook, Notification hook, and turn-lifecycle hooks are present in @samp{~/.claude/settings.json}. @code{agent-claude-ensure-state-hook-config} installs only the turn-lifecycle hooks listed in @code{agent-claude--state-hook-events}. The backend does not run setup automatically when loaded; run the setup command explicitly when installing or repairing hook configuration.

@node Codex commands
@section Codex commands

@findex agent-codex-start-or-switch
@findex agent-codex-select-account
@findex agent-codex-resume
@findex agent-codex-fork
The Codex commands expose workflows that are specific to the Codex CLI; handoff, restart, exit, skill execution, project audits, backtrace debugging, and Slack-message routing use the unified @code{agent-*} commands described above. @code{agent-codex-start-or-switch} starts Codex under the selected account when no Codex sessions exist, otherwise it opens the shared switcher. @code{agent-codex-select-account} persists an account choice by name and applies it to new interactive sessions and non-interactive @samp{codex exec} runs. Skills run non-interactively with @samp{codex exec}, using local skill metadata when available and falling back to a slash invocation otherwise; additional programmatic skill directories are configured through @code{agent-codex-programmatic-skill-directories}.

When @code{agent-restart} operates on a Codex session, it resumes codex.el's canonical session identity under the chosen account and current default @code{codex-terminal-backend}; app-server sessions use the native app-server resume path and terminal backends use @samp{codex resume}. Changing @code{codex-terminal-backend} and then restarting a Codex session switches the relaunched session to that backend. The unified commands cover both Codex resuming and Codex forking: @code{agent-resume} resumes a past session of whichever backend the context names, and @code{agent-create-branch} forks the current session of either backend, reaching Codex through @samp{codex fork} on the terminal backends and @samp{thread/fork} on the app-server backend. @code{agent-codex-resume} and @code{agent-codex-fork} still expose the CLI's own subcommands and remain available through @samp{M-x}, though neither is in @code{agent-menu} any more. @code{agent-codex-fork} is not redundant: it forks a session you pick from Codex's own history, whereas @code{agent-create-branch} forks the session you are sitting in.

