@findex agent-claude-status-context-percent
@findex agent-claude-status-token-count
@findex agent-claude-status-duration-ms
@findex agent-claude-status-session-usage
@findex agent-claude-status-weekly-usage
@findex agent-codex-status-model
@findex agent-codex-status-effort
@findex agent-codex-status-duration-ms
The Claude and Codex status helpers are intended for modeline integrations such as @samp{doom-modeline}. Claude status functions read the current session status JSON and expose model, reasoning effort, cost, context, duration, and token information. Claude usage functions expose account-level utilization returned by the Anthropic usage endpoint. Codex status functions expose the current session model, reasoning effort, and elapsed session duration; the model helper falls back to the configured model when the session has no live model value. When Codex has no explicit reasoning-effort override, the status helper reports Codex's default @samp{medium} effort. The session switcher uses backend busy, input-readiness, and background-work detectors to avoid showing actively responding sessions as waiting, and to distinguish truly idle waiting sessions from sessions that are accepting input while background terminals, agents, or tasks are still running. For Claude that evidence is the footer count — @samp{· N shells}, @samp{· N monitors}, or @samp{· ← N agents} — plus the Remote Control task UI@. A session counts as waiting only when it is blocked on the user, meaning it has stopped and will not proceed until the user submits something. Being able to queue text mid-turn is not waiting, since both CLIs accept typed input at any point. Codex app-server sessions learn turn boundaries from the JSON-RPC stream, so an inactive turn on a live app server is authoritative for waiting and an active turn is authoritative for busy, regardless of whether the prompt accepts input. Terminal sessions have no such signal and fall back to the session-event state machine and to scanning rendered status text. For Claude, @code{agent-claude--terminal-waiting-p} reports idle when the rendered tail shows the input prompt and no running-turn spinner line (a glyph, a verb ending in an ellipsis, and a parenthesised elapsed time, e.g. @samp{✢ Billowing… (4m 35s)}; the older @samp{esc to interrupt} hint and the background-task @samp{esc to give additional instructions} prompt also count as running). It is registered as the backend's @code{:idle-p} — not @code{:before-exit-ready-to-close-p}, whose veto runs on every chain step, where the screen's redraw lag behind the Stop hook would strand the chain — and consulted only by the before-exit chain and the escape interrupt check described in @ref{Session state machine}: when arming, a busy session whose terminal affirms idleness arms immediately, and the watchdog advances a chain stranded in @code{waiting-for-idle} whose stop event never arrived — a turn the CLI accepts and then drops, for instance at the context limit, fires no Stop hook. Screen scraping is not reliable enough to drive the switcher on its own, so display state stays with the session-event state machine; the interrupt check consults the screen only right after the user sends an escape. A @code{busy} state younger than @code{agent-claude-terminal-idle-grace} seconds is trusted over the screen, since the spinner takes a moment to appear after a submission. Codex prompt submission clears the previous waiting marker at the target buffer, so a session does not remain colored as waiting after the user sends a new prompt.

@node Transient menu
@chapter Transient menu

@findex agent-menu
@code{agent-menu} is a single static prefix defined in @samp{agent.el}, laid out in four columns: Sessions with Account beneath it, Current session, Tools with Prompts beneath it, and Options. Nothing in the layout depends on which backends are loaded, because every command in it is backend-agnostic. Each one resolves a backend from the current session buffer, or asks when nothing names one, and then dispatches through that backend's slots, so the same key operates on a Claude session and a Codex session alike. There is no longer any mechanism for a backend module to add entries to the menu, and this is deliberate: a backend contributes an implementation for a slot, and the command that calls that slot lives in @samp{agent.el}.

In the Options group, @samp{-c} selects an account. It acts on the current or prompted backend, while its value shows every registered backend's current account at once, so one entry replaces the per-backend account selectors the menu used to carry. The infix shares its implementation with the per-backend account commands in @samp{agent-account.el}, and syncs the selected account's config home as soon as the selection is made.

Boolean menu infixes use a shared transient class that toggles the bound variable each time it is invoked. The theme-sync infix additionally calls @code{agent-sync-theme-now} when enabling theme sync, so backend configuration files are updated immediately.

